<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mountstuart Elphinstone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mountstuart Elphinstone was a Scottish administrator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. As resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> he played a leading part in the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-maratha-war">Third Anglo-Maratha War</a> of 1817–1818 and then organised the conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> territories. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> from 1819 to 1827 he promoted education and a legal code, and he later wrote a well-known History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mountstuart-elphinstone.jpg" alt="Mountstuart Elphinstone" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountstuart_Elphinstone_1911.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
